Horizon knowledge file
======================

Field notes from building this. Mostly the Investec-specific things and the
forecasting judgement calls that cost real time, so the next person does not
have to re-learn them.


1. Sandbox and production use DIFFERENT hosts (this bites everyone)
   Sandbox serves BOTH the token and the data from
   openapisandbox.investec.com, token path /identity/v2/oauth2/token.
   Production serves both from openapi.investec.com (same paths, different
   host). Point sandbox code at the production host and every call fails auth
   while looking, from the outside, like an empty account. The two host sets
   live in vite.config.ts (HOSTS.sandbox / HOSTS.production) and in
   src-tauri/src/lib.rs. When a token call 401s or every data call 404s, check
   which host you are hitting first.


2. The transactions API talks RANDS, as plain numbers
   e.g. 8500 or 152.75, not cents. We keep the number exactly as the wire gives
   it and only attach a sign from the DEBIT/CREDIT type (investecAdapter.ts).
   Do not multiply by 100 "to be safe", you will be off by 100x.


3. The browser must NEVER hold the secret, and Investec sends no CORS headers
   So a pure frontend cannot call Investec directly on two counts. The fix is a
   tiny same-origin proxy that holds the credentials and forwards only the three
   read paths (/accounts, /accounts/{id}/balance, /accounts/{id}/transactions):
   - dev: a Vite middleware plugin (vite.config.ts)
   - packaged: a Tauri command (src-tauri/src/lib.rs, investec_read)
   The React adapter always calls /api/investec/* on its own origin and never
   learns the secret. User-supplied keys are POSTed to /api/investec/credentials
   and held in server memory only, never written to disk, never logged, never
   put in localStorage. Only the source label ("live") is persisted.


4. Vite reloads .env only on a SERVER restart
   loadEnv() runs when the plugin initialises, i.e. at server start. Editing
   .env while `npm run dev` is running does nothing until you restart it (or
   touch vite.config.ts, which makes Vite restart itself). Learned this the
   slow way while wiring up credentials.


5. Recurring detection: regularity beats volume
   Early versions flagged "Checkers" and "Uber" as recurring because they show
   up a lot. They are frequent but IRREGULAR. The fix (transactionService.ts)
   is to require 3+ occurrences AND a tight interval: score regularity as
   1 - spread/(cadence*0.5), drop anything under 0.6 regularity, and only then
   blend in volume. A real debit order lands on almost the same interval every
   month and sails through; noisy card spend does not. Confidence threshold
   0.75. This one change is the difference between a believable "Coming up" list
   and nonsense.


6. Forecast the MINIMUM, not just the endpoint
   For a scenario like "salary arrives 3 days late", the 30-day END balance is
   unchanged, the salary still lands inside the window. What changes is how low
   you dip BEFORE it lands. So the scenario comparison tracks the projected
   MINIMUM balance, which responds to timing changes, cutbacks and cancellations
   alike. Comparing endpoints made "salary late" look free, which is wrong.


7. Real sandbox data is messy; that is the point
   The sandbox "Mr Smith" account has hundreds of transactions with long, noisy
   descriptions (store numbers, city names). normaliseKey() strips digits and a
   few place words before grouping so repeats actually cluster. The forecast on
   real data drops from ~R33k to ~R11k over 30 days, a real trajectory, not a
   canned demo number. Keep the demo honest: show the assumptions, and say the
   forecast can be wrong.


8. Keep /check-style work off the hot path (relevant if you add card hooks)
   Not used here (Horizon is read-only), but if you extend it with the
   programmable card: the beforeTransaction hook has ~2 seconds and approves the
   spend itself if you are slow. Never put a bank call on that path.
